I stopped the obsolete run after it had already proved easy was solvable. Continuing would only lengthen the answer from 211 to 251 entries—the exact “grow the needle” failure the task forbids. I’m replacing it with a fixed-length Track B construction: Theorem 12 still supplies the native fall-coloring witness, but the matching is represented by a translation over a finite vector space. Generic Gaussian elimination costs \(O(n^3)\); a hidden rank-one update reduces it to under 300 exact operations, and modulus growth enlarges the answer space without adding answer elements.
